Solve the following pair of equations by substitution method:
\begin{align*}
7x - 15y &= 2 \quad (1) \\
x + 2y &= 3 \quad (2)
\end{align*}

pert_a:
\text{Step 1:} We pick either of the equations and write one variable in terms of the other. Let us consider Equation (2):
\begin{align*}
x + 2y &= 3 \\
x &= 3 - 2y \quad (3)
\end{align*}

\text{Step 2:} Substitute the value of \(x\) in Equation (1):
\begin{align*}
7(3 - 2y) - 15y &= 2 \\
21 - 14y - 15y &= 2 \\
21 - 29y &= 2 \\
-29y &= -19 \\
y &= \frac{19}{29}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{red}{\text{Step 3:} Substituting this value of \(y\) in Equation (1), we get:}
\begin{align*}
\textcolor{red}{7x - 15\left(\frac{19}{29}\right) = 2} \\
\textcolor{red}{7x - \frac{285}{29} = 2} \\
\textcolor{red}{7x = 2 + \frac{285}{29}} \\
\textcolor{red}{7x = \frac{343}{29}} \\
\textcolor{red}{x = \frac{49}{29}}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{red}{\text{Step 4:} Substituting this value of \(x\) in Equation (3), we get:}
\begin{align*}
\textcolor{red}{x &= 3 - 2\left(\frac{19}{29}\right) = \frac{49}{29}}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{red}{\text{Therefore, the solution is } x = \frac{49}{29}, \; y = \frac{19}{29}.}

