(i) Since the team will not include any girl, therefore, only boys are to be selected. \textcolor{red}{Therefore, the required number of ways is:}
\[
\binom{7}{5} = \frac{7!}{5! \, 2!} = \frac{6 \times 7}{2} = 21
\]
\textcolor{red}{5 boys out of 7 boys can be selected in \( \binom{7}{5} \) ways.}

(ii) Since at least one boy and one girl are to be there in every team, the team can consist of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item (a) 1 boy and 4 girls
    \item (b) 2 boys and 3 girls
    \item (c) 3 boys and 2 girls
    \item (d) 4 boys and 1 girl
\end{itemize}

- 1 boy and 4 girls can be selected in \( \binom{7}{1} \times \binom{4}{4} \) ways.
\textcolor{red}{Therefore, the required number of ways is:}
\[
\binom{7}{1} \times \binom{4}{4} + \binom{7}{2} \times \binom{4}{3} + \binom{7}{3} \times \binom{4}{2} + \binom{7}{4} \times \binom{4}{1} = 7 + 84 + 210 + 140 = 441
\]
\textcolor{red}{- 2 boys and 3 girls can be selected in \( \binom{7}{2} \times \binom{4}{3} \) ways.}
\textcolor{red}{- 3 boys and 2 girls can be selected in \( \binom{7}{3} \times \binom{4}{2} \) ways.}
\textcolor{red}{- 4 boys and 1 girl can be selected in \( \binom{7}{4} \times \binom{4}{1} \) ways.}

(iii) Since the team has to consist of at least 3 girls, the team can consist of:
- (a) 3 girls and 2 boys, or 
- (b) 4 girls and 1 boy.

Note that the team cannot have all 5 girls, because, the group has only 4 girls. \textcolor{red}{Therefore, the required number of ways is:}
\[
\binom{4}{3} \times \binom{7}{2} + \binom{4}{4} \times \binom{7}{1} = 84 + 7 = 91
\]
\textcolor{red}{3 girls and 2 boys can be selected in \( \binom{4}{3} \times \binom{7}{2} \) ways.}
\textcolor{red}{4 girls and 1 boy can be selected in \( \binom{4}{4} \times \binom{7}{1} \) ways.}

 Option C

A's 1 day's work = $\frac{1}{15}$; 

B's 1 day's work = $\frac{1}{20}$; 

(A + B)'s 1 day's work = $\left( \frac{1}{15} + \frac{1}{20} \right) = \frac{7}{60}$.

(A + B)'s 4 day's work = $\left( \frac{7}{60} \times 4 \right) = \frac{7}{15}$.

\textcolor{red}{Therefore, the work done by them together in 4 days = $\frac{7}{15}$.}


\[
\frac{1}{7 + 3\sqrt{2}} = \frac{1}{7 + 3\sqrt{2}} \times \frac{7 - 3\sqrt{2}}{7 - 3\sqrt{2}} 
\]
\[
= \frac{7 - 3\sqrt{2}}{(7 + 3\sqrt{2})(7 - 3\sqrt{2})} = \frac{7 - 3\sqrt{2}}{\textcolor{red}{49 - 6}} = \frac{7 - 3\sqrt{2}}{\textcolor{red}{43}}
\]

